// The committed set — 60 recall queries + 6 confidence controls, widened from
// 25 by T10.10. Fixture corpus only: no real prompt text, no real project,
// client or path appears in this file or in evals/fixture/. The private
// reference set lives in ~/.potsherd/evals/.
//
// ## why it is 60 and not 25
//
// The 25-query set could not decide what it was being asked to decide. Its
// verdict turned on the margin at recall@1, that margin was one query, and the
// binomial standard deviation of a 25-query count at p ~ 0.4 is about 2.4. A
// criterion decided by a margin smaller than its own noise is a coin flip with
// a rule attached. 60 queries do not make the instrument precise; they make a
// one-query difference stop being the deciding vote. `evals/QUERY-SET.md`
// records the construction, the per-query gold verification and the noise
// estimate.
//
// Every query is written the way somebody remembers a conversation weeks
// later: the thing it was *about*, not the words they typed at the time. The
// first version of this set failed that test — "webhook rate limited by the
// gateway" against a prompt saying "the outbound webhook is getting rate
// limited by their gateway" measures substring overlap, not retrieval, and
// scored 10/10 with every answer at rank 1 over eleven candidates. The runner
// prints the word overlap between each query and its answer so that failure
// cannot come back quietly; see `--overlap` in evals/run.ts.
//
// **No query in this set trips the 0.6 overlap flag.** Three that did in the
// 25-query set were rewritten rather than defended: the rrf query, the ci-disk
// ghost and the accessibility sidechain. What that costs is written down in
// QUERY-SET.md §5 — a short query whose distinctive word lives in the
// transcript is *structurally* a quotation, so the short-query class in this
// corpus can only be built out of card vocabulary.
//
// `class` records what each query is for:
//
//   concept   the answer shares almost no content words with the query. bm25
//             can genuinely fail here; this is where the vector half earns its
//             350 ms.
//   ranking   the words appear, and so do they in three or four distractor
//             sessions on adjacent topics. Getting it right is ordering.
//   recall    the ordinary case: a couple of distinctive words remembered
//             correctly, competing against a corpus that shares them.
//
// `needs` records *where in the index the answer physically is*:
//
//   sidechain  the words exist only in a subagent transcript; the parent
//              session never says what came back
//   ghost      the session was deleted. history.jsonl kept the prompts and
//              nothing else — there is no assistant text to match
//   card       the exchange text does not answer the question and the title
//              does not either. Only the card's summary does, which is the
//              "what was that session about" case
//   text       the ordinary exchange body
//
// The `note` of every query opens with a bracketed **form** tag, which is the
// second axis T10.10 added and the one QUERY-SET.md counts:
//
//   [keyword]        one to three distinctive words, typed the way a person
//                    types into a search box
//   [sentence]       a natural-language sentence or half-remembered phrase
//   [decision]       a question about a decision that was taken, not about a
//                    symptom that was observed
//   [cross-project]  the remembered topic occurs in two or more of the eight
//                    fixture projects, so the ranking has to discriminate
//                    across a project boundary and not merely within one
//
// A query can carry more than one tag; the counts in QUERY-SET.md are per tag
// and therefore sum to more than 60.
//
// `expected_sidechain` asserts the answer came back as the subagent
// transcript, not merely as some session whose id starts the same way — a
// claude sidechain id is `<parent>:agent-<hash>`, so a prefix test alone
// cannot tell the two apart.
//
// Every `expected_session_prefix` below was verified against the built index,
// not assumed: QUERY-SET.md §4 records the command and the output.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------ text
